{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Surface\nGitHub Actions, repo navapbc/rebar, workflow **Test Suite (mirror)** (scheduled/dispatch, non-blocking mirror tier), matrix `sweep (…)` jobs. Blocking core tiers pass on main; only the experimental `sweep` matrix is red.\n\n## Signatures (two distinct sub-failures)\n1. **sweep (windows)** — `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fcntl'` at collection; Windows tier cannot collect.\n2. **sweep (py3.14)** — `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py::test_a_contended_fcntl_leg_reads_as_held` fails `RuntimeError: Network access is forbidden in unit/scripts tests`.\n\n(The `sweep (macos, full suite)` / `sweep (declared lower-bound resolution)` legs are the recurrence of closed `e3e0-2e7c-b280-4b4a`, tracked there — not this ticket.)\n\n## Severity: P3\nMirror/experimental tier — non-blocking. CI-hygiene / cross-platform-portability signal.\n\n---\n\n## Fix plan (Phase 2) — two independent root causes, confirmed in Phase 1 RCA comment\n\n**RC#1 (Windows collection blackout):** 4 modules do an *unconditional* module-top `import fcntl` — `src/rebar/_store/lock.py`, `src/rebar/_store/git_locking.py`, `src/rebar/reducer/marker.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py`. Because `import rebar` transitively hits `doctor_locks.py`, ALL collection dies on non-POSIX. The codebase already establishes that a module-top fcntl import must be made *conditional*, and does so with **three distinct legitimate shapes** (none is a single uniform pattern):\n  - **module-top `try/except ImportError → fcntl = None`** — `_snapshot/janitor.py`, `_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`, `_snapshot/git_fetch.py`, `rebar_reconciler/alert_store.py`'s sibling guarded modules;\n  - **`if sys.platform != \"win32\": import fcntl` / `else: fcntl = None`** — `_engine/rebar_reconciler/alert_store.py:12-15`;\n  - **function-local (lazy) `import fcntl`** — `_commands/recent_creates.py:64` (inside `_journal_lock`), `_store/hlc.py:106` (inside `_hlc_lock`); a deferred import never runs at collection time, so it never breaks collection.\n\nFix = guard the 4 unconditional imports with the module-top `try/except ImportError → None` shape (collectability only; Windows fcntl *locking* is out of scope — non-support-target). On non-POSIX, `fcntl` binds to `None`, so a subsequent `fcntl.flock(...)` call site inside those 4 modules would raise `AttributeError` at call time — that is acceptable and in-scope-excluded: Windows is not a declared support target and these are POSIX-only lock paths; the deliverable is import/collection success, not Windows locking. Zero behavior change on POSIX (fcntl stays the real module).\n\n**RC#2 (py3.14 red):** the autouse `_network_guard` (`tests/conftest.py`) patches **two** seams: `patch.object(socket.socket, \"connect\", _guard_socket_connect)` AND `patch(\"socket.create_connection\", side_effect=RuntimeError(...))`. The `connect` seam rejected **all** address families; py3.14's Linux default start-method change `fork`→`forkserver` makes `multiprocessing` reach its server over an **AF_UNIX** (local IPC) connect, which the all-families guard wrongly rejects. Fix = narrow the **`connect`** seam only: block just `AF_INET`/`AF_INET6` (real network) and delegate every other family (AF_UNIX etc.) to the captured original `socket.socket.connect`. The **`create_connection`** seam is left AS-IS — it is a real-TCP-only helper (always builds an AF_INET/AF_INET6 socket), so it needs no family check and blocking it wholesale stays correct.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — `import fcntl` guarded (module-top `try/except ImportError → None`); POSIX behavior unchanged.\n- [x] `src/rebar/_store/git_locking.py` — `import fcntl` guarded.\n- [x] `src/rebar/reducer/marker.py` — `import fcntl` guarded.\n- [x] `src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py` — `import fcntl` guarded (the leaf that breaks `import rebar`).\n- [x] RED→GREEN: the 4 modules import under simulated non-POSIX (`sys.modules['fcntl']=None`) and `import rebar` succeeds — proven by `pytest tests/unit/test_fcntl_import_guard.py`.\n- [x] Structural guard `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` (AST; stdlib-only, wired into `make lint`) fails only on a genuinely **unconditional** module-top `import fcntl` in `src/rebar`. It treats an import as guarded when it is (a) inside a `try`, (b) inside an `if`/`else` (the `sys.platform` conditional), OR (c) function-local (never module-top). A `# fcntl-guard-ok: <reason>` marker is the sanctioned escape hatch. Ships true-positive + true-negative (one case for EACH of the three live shapes — try/except like `git_fetch.py`, `sys.platform` like `alert_store.py`, function-local like `hlc.py`) + refactor-survival tests — proven by `python scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` and its test.\n- [x] Step7 test siblings `tests/unit/test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py` and `tests/unit/test_mcp_entrypoint_provisioning.py` use `pytest.importorskip(\"fcntl\")` so they skip (not error) on non-POSIX.\n- [x] `tests/conftest.py` — the `connect` seam blocks only AF_INET/AF_INET6 and delegates other families to the real connect; the `create_connection` seam is unchanged — proven by `pytest tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py`.\n- [x] RED→GREEN: a `forkserver` `multiprocessing.Process` starts under the guard; an AF_UNIX connect is allowed; a real AF_INET connect is still blocked (teeth).\n\n## Testing\n- `python scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` — the new structural guard (also via `make lint`).\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_fcntl_import_guard.py tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py` — the two RED→GREEN regressions.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` — original-report replication (under forkserver).\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` — clean.\n\n### Portability (project.portability)\nThe structural guard is a stdlib-only AST script wired into `make lint` — no CI provider required, runnable locally and under any CI. The network-guard narrowing is interpreter/OS-agnostic (a socket-family check).\n\n## Provenance\nPeriodic ops-error sweep. `caused_by` → `13ad-4c6f-4074-4f43` (added both red tiers). See Phase 1 RCA comment for full mechanism proofs + attribution."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788158997344905001,"uuid":"d5995f09-c305-4486-8200-a138177768dd"}